  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 254 AM EDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\n===== Rain Chances Return Thursday =====\n\nUpper ridging will continue to be quite strong across the western \nUS, with downstream troughing set up across the eastern half of the \ncountry. A cold front will dive southward through the Ohio Valley \nlate Wednesday and into Thursday, which will bring our next chance \nfor showers and maybe a few storms. Model soundings do not show very \nfavorable parameters for storms, with weak shear and meager lapse \nrates keeping severe potential low. Coverage also looks to be rather \nisolated, so not expecting this precip chance to be widespread, and \ncould end up with most of the area remaining mostly dry. PoPs are \nlimited to 20-30% chance Wednesday night and Thursday. \n\nAdditionally, we'll see increasing moisture for Thursday ahead of \nthe frontal boundary, with dewpoints approaching 70F. Temps are \nforecast to top out in the upper 80s and near 90 on Thursday, so \nit'll feel a little more muggy than in previous days. Drier weather \nreturns for Friday behind the frontal passage, with lower dewpoints \nagain too. \n\n\n===== Dry Weather Expected Over the Weekend =====\n\nThe upper trough will weaken by the weekend, leading to the upper \npattern to flatten out some, though we'll see increasing heights \nacross our area. Sfc high pressure will take control again, leading \nto a dry weekend, and warming temps that could be the precursor to \nmuch warmer temps into early next week. Temps Saturday and Sunday \ncould peak in the low 90s. By early next week, stronger ridging will \nset up over the eastern half of the US, leading to continued dry \nconditions, and perhaps a warm up in daily temps. NBM probs for \ntemps greater than 90F exceed 75-80% for early next week.",
